(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-2-cardiovascular-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Advanced Cardiovascular Examination Quiz 2 — PD2 Exam 2","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is Homan's sign elicited, and what is a positive result?","choices":["Quick ankle dorsiflexion, knee bent; pain behind it","Compression of both wrist arteries; delayed hand filling","Thumb pressure for two seconds; a persisting depression","Simultaneous radial and femoral palpation; a delay"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stem asks for both halves of the sign, and this option gives both: quickly and forcefully dorsiflex the foot at the ankle while keeping the knee bent, and a positive result is pain behind the knee during the maneuver. It is the test that checks for deep vein thrombosis, and a positive result has traditionally been thought of as a clinical sign of it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"What usually causes a systolic click?","choices":["Pericarditis","Mitral valve prolapse","Aortic valve stenosis","Mitral stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — abnormal systolic ballooning of part of the valve into the left atrium, from leaflet redundancy and elongation of the chordae tendineae. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What should the examiner attend to about their hands?","choices":["Long nails for precise palpation","Warm hands and short nails","Gloved hands at all times","Cold hands to provoke vasoconstriction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside a quiet room and adequate lighting — small things that determine whether the patient stays relaxed enough for the findings to be real. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which valves are open during ventricular systole?","choices":["None of them","The pulmonic and aortic valves","The tricuspid and mitral valves","All four valves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary arteries and the left into the aorta, while the tricuspid and mitral valves are closed. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Where does the murmur of pulmonic stenosis radiate?","choices":["To the left axilla","To the right sternum and xiphoid","Toward the left shoulder and neck, if loud","To the carotids and down the left sternal border"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis is a midsystolic, crescendo-decrescendo, medium-pitched harsh murmur heard at the pulmonic area, and when it is loud it radiates toward the left shoulder and neck. The 'if loud' qualifier matters: a loud pulmonic stenosis murmur also brings a thrill, and no maneuvers are listed for it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What should be remembered about palpitations?","choices":["They are the most common symptom of coronary disease","They are only significant if they last over an hour","They do not necessarily mean heart disease","They always indicate an arrhythmia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — anxious and hyperthyroid patients may report them. The instruction attached is to get an electrocardiogram and to reword your questions if needed. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How is the ankle brachial index calculated?","choices":["Brachial systolic over the higher ankle pressure","Lower of the two ankle pressures over the brachial systolic","Average of both ankle pressures over the average brachial","Higher ankle pressure over brachial systolic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, calculated for each leg and recorded to two decimal places. The normal range is 0.90 to 1.40. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What produces a pathologic S3?","choices":["Turbulence across a narrowed mitral valve","High filling pressures, abruptly decelerating inflow","Atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle","Sudden halting of a semilunar valve as it opens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The stem asks about the pathologic S3, the one heard in adults over 40. High left ventricular filling pressures and abrupt deceleration of inflow across the mitral valve at the end of rapid filling produce it, like blood slapping against the ventricular wall; heart failure and volume overload from aortic or mitral regurgitation are among its causes. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which of these is a named cause of a murmur?","choices":["Inflammation of the pericardium","Reduced ventricular compliance","High left ventricular filling pressure","Ejection into a dilated chamber"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ejection into a dilated chamber is one of the five named causes of a murmur, the sound of turbulent blood flow that sounds like a 'swoosh'. The other four are flow across a partially obstructed valve, increased flow through a normal valve in a small child, regurgitant flow across an incompetent valve, and abnormal shunting into a lower pressure chamber. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which four diagnoses should always be considered with chest pain?","choices":["Angina, infarction, dissection, embolism","Pericarditis, pleurisy, esophagitis and gastritis","Anxiety, hyperthyroidism, anemia and pregnancy","Heart failure, valve disease, arrhythmia and cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and beyond them the task is distinguishing cardiovascular causes from pericardium, trachea, bronchi, parietal pleura, esophagus, chest wall, gallbladder, stomach and neck. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What happens to mitral valve prolapse on squatting or leg raise?","choices":["It is moved later in systole","It becomes louder and earlier","It disappears entirely","It becomes a diastolic murmur"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the increased ventricular volume delays the point at which the redundant leaflet balloons into the atrium. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What are the findings of chronic venous insufficiency?","choices":["Cool skin with decreased pulses and no edema","Foot pallor on elevation with dusky rubor","Mild or no pain, normal temperature, pitting edema","Painful ulceration with thin shiny skin and hair loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic venous insufficiency brings a normal skin temperature and pitting edema, and the limb is listed with no pain; the only pain named is the small, painful granulation tissue inside a venous ulcer, which is why 'mild or no pain' fits. The rest of the picture: brownish hemosiderin staining, thickened skin, normal pulses, and gangrene that is rare. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How is the carotid examined?","choices":["Auscultate first, then palpate one side at a time","Palpate both sides simultaneously to compare","Palpate first, then auscultate","Auscultate while the patient breathes normally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with the patient holding their breath, lateral to the trachea. Palpating both at once risks compromising cerebral flow. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"How do diastolic murmurs compare with systolic ones?","choices":["Always pathologic and always loud","Less common and more difficult to hear","More common and easier to hear","Equally common but louder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diastolic murmurs are both less common than systolic murmurs and more difficult to hear, which answers both halves of the comparison the stem asks for. That is why they need deliberate technique: aortic regurgitation, for example, is grade 1 to 3, high-pitched and heard best with the patient sitting, leaning forward and holding the breath after exhaling. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How do aortic and pulmonic ejection sounds differ with respiration?","choices":["Both increase with inspiration","Both disappear with inspiration","The aortic does not vary; the pulmonic decreases with inspiration","The aortic decreases with inspiration; the pulmonic does not vary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and their locations differ too — aortic at the base and apex, pulmonic best in the second and third intercostal spaces. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What should a radial to femoral pulse lag prompt you to consider?","choices":["Chronic venous insufficiency","Mitral valve prolapse","Coarctation of the aorta","Deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The stem's finding is a lag between the radial and femoral pulses, which you look for by palpating both at the same time. When that lag is present, the condition to consider is coarctation of the aorta. The femoral pulse used for this lies below the inguinal ligament, midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which sounds is the bell better for?","choices":["High-pitched sounds","Both equally","Only murmurs, not heart sounds","Low-pitched sounds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — S3, S4 and the murmur of mitral stenosis. It is applied lightly to the skin, ensuring no gaps, at the apex and moving medially along the lower sternal border. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where is the right ventricular impulse felt?","choices":["Fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Second intercostal space at the right sternal border","Over the carotid arteries","The 3rd to 5th left intercostal spaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The right ventricular impulse is felt in the 3rd, 4th and 5th left intercostal spaces, and with pressure overload it may extend to the subxiphoid process. With volume overload, as in atrial septal defect, it lies along the left sternal border toward the left cardiac border; unlike the left ventricular impulse, its diameter is not useful. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is a patient asked to perform a Valsalva?","choices":["Grip both hands tightly for thirty seconds","Lean forward and exhale completely","Bear down as if straining","Squat quickly from standing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ask the supine patient to bear down as if straining during a bowel movement, or to push against your hand on the mid-abdomen. This forceful expiration against a closed airway raises intrathoracic pressure and decreases left ventricular filling, so a hypertrophic cardiomyopathy murmur increases while an aortic stenosis murmur gets softer or does not change. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Where is the brachial pulse found?","choices":["At the lateral portion of the wrist","Behind the medial malleolus","Medial to the biceps tendon at the elbow","Lateral to the biceps tendon at the elbow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The brachial pulse is felt medial to the biceps tendon at the antecubital fossa, which is the landmark the stem is asking for. Palpating on the medial side of the tendon, not lateral to it, is the detail that finds the artery. Farther down the same limb, the radial pulse is at the lateral portion of the wrist and the ulnar pulse at the medial portion. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What produces the second heart sound?","choices":["Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves","Atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle","Turbulent flow across a narrowed valve","Closure of the pulmonic and aortic valves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. S2 comes at the start of diastole: as the ventricles start to relax, the pulmonic and aortic valves close, and that closure is the second heart sound. It has two parts, aortic (A2), usually louder because of the high pressure in the aorta, and pulmonic (P2), normally fused on expiration and audibly split on inspiration. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When does paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea typically occur?","choices":["Immediately on lying down","In the early morning on waking","Only during exertion","One to two hours after going to bed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — episodes of sudden dyspnea and orthopnea that wake the patient, prompting them to sit up, stand, or go to a window for air. Wheezing and coughing may accompany it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What causes a pericardial friction rub?","choices":["Reduced ventricular compliance","Inflammation of the pericardial sac","Turbulent flow across a stenotic valve","Regurgitant flow across an incompetent valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pericardial friction rub comes from inflammation of the pericardial sac, that is, pericarditis. It is a scratchy, scraping noise heard intermittently through both diastole and systole, high-pitched and best heard with the diaphragm, most often at Erb's point, and it may increase when the patient leans forward, exhales and holds the breath. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What is normal capillary refill time?","choices":["Two to three seconds","Under one second","Five to six seconds","It varies too much to have a normal value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, tested by pressing on the fingernail and noting how long the nail bed takes to become pink again. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the cardiac cycle?","choices":["The interval between S1 and S2","From the start of one heartbeat to the next","The period of ventricular contraction only","The period of ventricular relaxation only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cardiac cycle is the period between the start of one heartbeat and the beginning of the next, made of alternating periods of contraction and relaxation. It is divided into two phases, systole (ventricular contraction) and diastole (ventricular relaxation and filling), so an option naming only one phase describes part of the cycle, not the whole. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What position brings out aortic regurgitation?","choices":["Sitting forward, breath held after exhaling","Left lateral decubitus with the bell","Supine while bearing down","Standing quickly from a squat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the same position is used for the pericardial friction rub — both are brought closer to the chest wall by it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What does a diffuse ventricular impulse indicate?","choices":["Ventricular hypertrophy from increased afterload","A transient rise in stroke volume","Constriction of the pericardium","Dilation from chronic volume overload"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — increased preload, with aortic or mitral regurgitation and cardiomyopathy as the examples. The impulse is displaced left and possibly downward. That is increased preload. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why is the murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy singled out as important to learn?","choices":["Because it radiates to the carotids","So a sports physical is not signed off incorrectly","Because it is the commonest murmur in adults","Because it always produces a thrill"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is crescendo-decrescendo and midsystolic along the left sternal border, and missing it in a young athlete has obvious consequences. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which questions help characterize edema?","choices":["Do shoes or rings get tight? Eyelids puffy?","How far can you walk before the pain stops you?","How many pillows do you sleep on?","Does the sensation feel like skipping or fluttering?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Edema questions focus on the location, timing and setting of the swelling and on associated symptoms, and tight shoes, tight rings and puffy eyelids in the morning ask exactly where and when the fluid collects. Asking whether it is worse in the morning or the evening, and suggesting a daily morning weight, round out the history. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Where is the point of maximal impulse normally found?","choices":["Fifth space, at or just medial to midclavicular","Second intercostal space at the right sternal border","Fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border","Sixth intercostal space at the anterior axillary line"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it identifies the LEFT BORDER of the heart — which is why a change in cardiac size or shape moves it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"}]};
